+# Deploying the USDC Tip Jar
+
+Two artifacts ship: the `TipJar` contract on **Base mainnet**, and the frontend as a **static export
+pinned on IPFS**. The local development workflow is in [README.md](./README.md).
+
+Nothing in this document has been executed — no contract has been deployed to Base and no bundle has
+been uploaded. These are the exact commands to run, in order.
+
+---
+
+## 1. Deploy `TipJar` to Base
+
+The frontend cannot be built for Base until the contract exists there: `yarn deploy` regenerates
+`packages/nextjs/contracts/deployedContracts.ts`, and the next step will not typecheck without the
+`8453` entry. That ordering is deliberate.
+
+```bash
+# One-time: create or import the deployer keystore (never the default anvil account on a live chain)
+yarn generate          # or: yarn account:import
+yarn account           # confirm the address and that it holds ETH on Base
+
+yarn deploy --network base
+```
+
+Use the plain form, not `--file DeployTipJar.s.sol`. `script/Deploy.s.sol` wraps `DeployTipJar`, and
+`script/VerifyAll.s.sol` reads `broadcast/Deploy.s.sol/<chainid>/run-latest.json` by name — deploying
+through the `--file` form writes `broadcast/DeployTipJar.s.sol/...` instead and the verify step below
+cannot find it.
+
+`DeployTipJar.s.sol` accepts chain id `8453` and `31337` only, and asserts USDC has code at
+`0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913` before deploying. The `TipJar` owner is the deployer —
+the only account that can call `withdraw(address)`.
+
+Verify the source on Basescan (`foundry.toml` already carries a `base` entry under `[etherscan]`;
+set a real `ETHERSCAN_API_KEY` in `packages/foundry/.env` first):
+
+```bash
+yarn verify --network base
+```
+
+Record the deployed address; sanity-check it against the real token before announcing anything:
+
+```bash
+cast call <TIPJAR_ADDRESS> "token()(address)" --rpc-url https://mainnet.base.org
+# expect 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913
+
+cast call <TIPJAR_ADDRESS> "owner()(address)" --rpc-url https://mainnet.base.org
+```
+
+## 2. Point the frontend at Base
+
+In `packages/nextjs/scaffold.config.ts`:
+
+```diff
+-const targetNetwork = chains.foundry;
++const targetNetwork = chains.base;
+```
+
+This is a single literal rather than an environment variable on purpose. The scaffold hooks derive
+`ContractName` from the configured chain, so if `TipJar` is missing from the `8453` entry in
+`deployedContracts.ts`, `yarn build` fails to typecheck. Step 1 not being done is caught at build
+time instead of shipping a page that renders "No TipJar on Base".
+
+Set a dedicated Base RPC. Left unset, the bundle falls back to viem's public Base endpoint plus
+Scaffold-ETH's shared demo Alchemy key — and every visitor to the CID shares them:
+
+```bash
+export NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_RPC_URL="https://base-mainnet.g.alchemy.com/v2/<YOUR_KEY>"
+# or, with your own Alchemy key, leave the override unset and set:
+export NEXT_PUBLIC_ALCHEMY_API_KEY="<YOUR_KEY>"
+```
+
+The client batches contract reads through Multicall3 (`services/web3/wagmiConfig.tsx`), so the page's
+watched reads cost roughly one call per block rather than one per read — but the endpoint is still
+public, baked into an immutable bundle, and impossible to rotate without a new CID.
+
+## 3. Build the static bundle
+
+```bash
+cd packages/nextjs
+rm -rf .next out
+
+NEXT_PUBLIC_PRODUCTION_URL="https://<your-production-domain>" \
+  NEXT_PUBLIC_IPFS_BUILD=true \
+  yarn build
+```
+
+- `NEXT_PUBLIC_IPFS_BUILD=true` switches `next.config.ts` to `output: "export"`, `trailingSlash: true`
+  (so every route becomes its own directory with an `index.html`), and `images: { unoptimized: true }`.
+- `NEXT_PUBLIC_PRODUCTION_URL` is the origin Open Graph URLs and the preview image are built from.
+  Without it they bake in `http://localhost:3000` and every share card breaks. Use the domain the site
+  will actually be served from — the ENS gateway domain from step 6, e.g. `https://tipjar.eth.limo`.
+- No `NODE_OPTIONS` juggling is needed. `packages/nextjs/scripts/run-next.mjs` adds
+  `--no-experimental-webstorage` for Node 25 at the process level so the build workers inherit it;
+  see the Node 25 note in the README.
+
+## 4. Verify the bundle before uploading
+
+```bash
+cd packages/nextjs
+
+# Every route emitted its own directory with an index.html
+ls out/*/index.html
+find out -name index.html | sort
+
+# The tip jar page is actually in there — not a stale or default build
+grep -o "USDC Tip Jar" out/index.html
+
+# Open Graph URLs carry the production origin, not localhost
+grep -o 'property="og:image" content="[^"]*"' out/index.html
+grep -rl "localhost:3000" out/ || echo "no localhost leaks"
+```
+
+`ls out/*/index.html` lists the subdirectory routes (`404`, `_not-found`, `blockexplorer`, `debug`);
+`find` additionally shows `out/index.html` for the tip jar itself and the two prerendered block
+explorer detail routes. If a route you expect is missing, `output: "export"` silently skipped it —
+fix that before uploading.
+
+Then load the bundle locally, from the root of an origin, before it goes anywhere:
+
+```bash
+cd out && python3 -m http.server 8099 --bind 127.0.0.1
+# then, in another terminal:
+curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" -L "http://127.0.0.1:8099/"
+curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" -L "http://127.0.0.1:8099/debug/"
+```
+
+Open http://127.0.0.1:8099 in a browser and confirm the feed loads from Base and the console is clean.
+A build that only 200s on curl can still be broken at runtime.
+
+## 5. Upload to IPFS
+
+```bash
+cd packages/nextjs
+yarn bgipfs upload config init -u https://upload.bgipfs.com
+yarn bgipfs upload out
+```
+
+The command prints `CID: <cid>` — **record it**. The repo also ships a one-shot
+`yarn ipfs` (from the repo root) that builds and uploads in a single step, but it does not set
+`NEXT_PUBLIC_PRODUCTION_URL`, so use the explicit build in step 3 followed by the upload above.
+
+> If the CID is unchanged from a previous upload, the uploaded bytes are byte-for-byte identical.
+> That means the rebuild did not take — check `out/` and the upload target before blaming gateway
+> caching.
+
+Pin the CID somewhere durable (your own node, or a pinning service) so it survives gateway garbage
+collection.
+
+## 6. Verify the deployed site
+
+**Use a subdomain gateway or an ENS domain, not a path gateway.** The Next.js static export
+references assets with root-absolute paths (`/_next/static/...`), so at
+`https://ipfs.io/ipfs/<cid>/` the HTML loads but every script 404s. Serving the CID from the root of
+an origin is a requirement, not a preference.
+
+```bash
+CID=<cid>
+
+# Root and at least one non-home route. Root succeeding does not prove exported routes resolve.
+curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" -L "https://$CID.ipfs.dweb.link/"
+curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" -L "https://$CID.ipfs.dweb.link/debug/"   # expect 200, not 404
+
+# The Open Graph image and URL use the production origin
+curl -s -L "https://$CID.ipfs.dweb.link/" | grep -o 'property="og:[^"]*" content="[^"]*"'
+
+# An asset resolves, which is what a path gateway would break
+curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" -L "https://$CID.ipfs.dweb.link/thumbnail.jpg"
+```
+
+Then open the subdomain-gateway URL in a browser and confirm the tip feed loads from Base, the
+connect button works, and the console is clean.
+
+## 7. Point ENS at the CID
+
+Only after the CID above has been reviewed and approved:
+
+1. Set the ENS name's **content hash** record to `ipfs://<cid>`.
+2. Read the record back from the resolver rather than trusting the transaction:
+
+   ```bash
+   ETH_RPC=https://ethereum-rpc.publicnode.com
+   NAME=<your-name>.eth
+   NODE=$(cast namehash "$NAME")
+
+   # ENS registry -> the resolver actually serving this name
+   RESOLVER=$(cast call 0x00000000000C2E074eC69A0dFb2997BA6C7d2e1e \
+     "resolver(bytes32)(address)" "$NODE" --rpc-url "$ETH_RPC")
+
+   # The stored content hash. `0xe30101701220...` is ipfs-ns / CIDv1 / dag-pb / sha2-256,
+   # followed by the 32-byte digest of the CID you just approved.
+   cast call "$RESOLVER" "contenthash(bytes32)(bytes)" "$NODE" --rpc-url "$ETH_RPC"
+   ```
+
+   An empty `0x` means the record did not land.
+3. Confirm the public gateway serves that CID through the name:
+
+   ```bash
+   curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" -L "https://<your-name>.eth.limo/"
+   curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" -L "https://<your-name>.eth.limo/debug/"
+   ```
+
+4. Confirm `NEXT_PUBLIC_PRODUCTION_URL` from step 3 matches this domain. If it does not, rebuild,
+   re-upload, and update the content hash to the new CID.
+
+The release is not done until steps 4, 6, and 7 all pass.
+
+---
+
+## Rollback
+
+IPFS content is immutable, so rolling back is repointing the ENS content hash at the previous CID.
+Keep the last known-good CID with the commit it was built from.
+
+## Withdrawing tips
+
+Only the `TipJar` owner (the deployer from step 1) can move the collected USDC:
+
+```bash
+cast send <TIPJAR_ADDRESS> "withdraw(address)" <RECIPIENT> \
+  --account <YOUR_KEYSTORE> --rpc-url https://mainnet.base.org
+```
+
+Rehearse it on the fork first — `yarn fork --network base`, `yarn deploy`, `yarn fund`, send a tip,
+then withdraw. The call reverts with `NothingToWithdraw()` on an empty jar.
